title: relay dm poll 403 signature_invalid for synthetic cli-<alias> session; recurs after connector restart
updated_at: "2026-07-17T17:07:38Z"
---
During cross-host dogfood (2026-07-18): 'c2c relay dm poll --alias kimi-suvi-lumo-9cr1' returned 403 signature_invalid: 'verified signer kimi-suvi-lumo-9cr1 does not own session (cli-kimi-suvi-lumo-9cr1, cli-kimi-suvi-lumo-9cr1)' — while 'relay dm send' with the same alias was accepted (asymmetric enforcement). 'c2c relay register --alias ...' fixed poll immediately, but the 403 RETURNED after 'c2c restart relay-connect' (~10 min later), suggesting the connector's re-sync invalidates the synthetic cli-<alias> session binding. Expected: CLI relay ops should use a durable binding (register the synthetic session on first use and keep it across connector syncs), or send should enforce the same ownership check as poll so the failure is uniform.

Mechanism identified by kimi-garnet-wolf-7ndt@b86cae1658da (independent diagnosis, matches observation): the relay lease is SESSION-scoped and the running relay-connect owns it; the CLI derives a synthetic 'cli-<alias>' session that does not own the lease, so poll/peek 403 while send is accepted. Critically, the hint's suggested fix ('c2c relay register') makes things WORSE: re-registering steals the lease from the connector and the two fight over it — on xsm the connector wedged (~B228 recurrence) within ~10 min of a manual re-register. Garnet worked around by making its inbox cron local-only. Fix direction: CLI relay poll/peek should route through the connector's lease (or share its session binding), and the error hint should stop recommending re-register when a live connector owns the lease.